WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:08.129
It is not an unusual sight in Iran:

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:11.758
a large conference attended only by men.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.596
After all, women are to be hidden
from public view,

00:00:16.638 --> 00:00:20.767
kept uneducated, silent, powerless.

00:00:22.227 --> 00:00:26.356
It is within these strict confines
that a young woman,

00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:31.820
eyes bright with possibility
for a more just and equitable world,

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:37.492
bravely steps into this gathering of men
with her veil removed.

00:00:38.702 --> 00:00:41.329
Her face is uncovered,

00:00:41.371 --> 00:00:44.874
an act considered blasphemous.

00:00:44.916 --> 00:00:50.171
Just the presence of this woman alone
sends shock waves through the crowd.

00:00:51.965 --> 00:00:57.762
She declares that the time has come
to break away from antiquated traditions

00:00:57.846 --> 00:01:02.559
and step into a new era
of human consciousness,

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:07.856
in which every person is free
to choose their own path.

00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:12.944
Soon after, she is imprisoned,

00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:17.866
charged with spreading corruption on Earth

00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:19.868
and sentenced to death.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.706
This woman was Táhirih.

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:31.087
A poet, a mystic,
a champion for human rights.

00:01:32.255 --> 00:01:37.844
Now, this story may sound
like a recent headline from Iran,

00:01:37.886 --> 00:01:43.725
but it actually took place
nearly 200 years ago, in 1848.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.856
When I look at my birthplace
of Iran today, I have to ask,

00:01:49.898 --> 00:01:51.399
what's changed?

00:01:51.441 --> 00:01:53.443
Have we made any progress?

00:01:54.402 --> 00:02:00.241
I see Táhirih's fate being replayed
in the lives of other courageous women

00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:04.537
who at this very moment
are choosing nonviolent means,

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:08.208
such as speaking out
and removing their hijabs,

00:02:08.249 --> 00:02:10.210
to proclaim their freedoms.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:15.882
And tragically, just like Táhirih,

00:02:15.924 --> 00:02:18.927
their lives and the rights
they are defending

00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:22.097
are being stamped out and suffocated.

00:02:23.098 --> 00:02:25.642
It's easy to feel hopeless.

00:02:26.559 --> 00:02:28.436
It's easy to feel defeated.

00:02:30.605 --> 00:02:34.067
Yet within their stories,

00:02:34.109 --> 00:02:35.652
and within hers,

00:02:35.694 --> 00:02:37.737
I do see progress.

00:02:38.405 --> 00:02:41.574
I see powerful lessons about what it means

00:02:41.616 --> 00:02:46.413
to believe so deeply
in the transformation of society

00:02:46.454 --> 00:02:51.668
that we dedicate our lives to laying down
the building blocks of a better world.

00:02:52.502 --> 00:02:54.879
A world we may not even live to see.

00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:02.345
Táhirih's life is an example
of being undeterred

00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:04.848
by our limitations and our constraints.

00:03:06.516 --> 00:03:10.186
In an arranged marriage
since the age of 14,

00:03:10.228 --> 00:03:12.397
and with three children,

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:16.985
Táhirih still used whatever tools
she had within her means --

00:03:17.026 --> 00:03:19.779
her words, her actions, her poetry --

00:03:19.821 --> 00:03:25.076
to encourage all of those around her
to abandon the suffocating scripts

00:03:25.118 --> 00:03:28.413
that they had inherited for their lives,

00:03:28.455 --> 00:03:33.334
and to independently investigate
the truth for themselves.

00:03:35.503 --> 00:03:38.673
She was met with hostile opposition,

00:03:38.715 --> 00:03:42.093
abuse and imprisonment.

00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:45.764
Yet Táhirih was unflinching
in her efforts,

00:03:45.805 --> 00:03:51.853
fixing her eyes on a horizon line
of justice and freedom for all.

00:03:54.564 --> 00:03:59.360
Through Táhirih's actions,
we understand the power that sacrifice has

00:03:59.402 --> 00:04:03.031
in awakening a collective consciousness.

00:04:03.072 --> 00:04:07.452
Though much of her writing and her poetry
was destroyed after her death,

00:04:07.494 --> 00:04:09.662
her legacy could not be extinguished.

00:04:10.538 --> 00:04:15.752
So many hearts were transformed
by the teachings that she spread,

00:04:15.794 --> 00:04:21.508
and the seeds of possibility
that she planted for equality and freedom

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:24.552
are now bursting through the soil of Iran,

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:29.307
as evidenced in these brave, youthful,
female-led protests

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:34.687
that erupted following the tragic death
of Mahsa Jina Amini,

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:37.899
the 22-year-old Kurdish Iranian woman

00:04:37.941 --> 00:04:41.277
who died in police custody
after being arrested

00:04:41.319 --> 00:04:44.155
for improperly wearing her hijab.

00:04:46.658 --> 00:04:53.665
Now today, when I hear that rallying cry,
"woman, life, freedom,"

00:04:53.832 --> 00:04:57.919
I can't help but hear Táhirih's last words

00:04:57.961 --> 00:05:00.672
before she was executed,

00:05:00.713 --> 00:05:05.009
taken in the middle of the night
and strangled with her own scarf.

00:05:05.927 --> 00:05:07.178
She said,

00:05:08.096 --> 00:05:11.474
"You can kill me as soon as you like,

00:05:11.516 --> 00:05:15.019
but you cannot stop
the emancipation of women."

00:05:16.604 --> 00:05:19.732
"You can kill me as soon as you like,

00:05:19.774 --> 00:05:23.695
but you cannot stop
the emancipation of women."

00:05:26.781 --> 00:05:30.034
Táhirih's legacy
invites each of us to consider

00:05:30.076 --> 00:05:32.245
what is fueling our own activism.

00:05:33.204 --> 00:05:36.624
Her words and actions,
though radical and upending,

00:05:36.666 --> 00:05:41.796
were not born out of bitterness
or hatred, or fueled by self-interest.

00:05:42.964 --> 00:05:48.553
It was Táhirih's deep love for humanity
that propelled her forward.

00:05:49.929 --> 00:05:52.098
And therein lies our challenge.

00:05:53.308 --> 00:05:58.062
Can we stand up
against oppression and injustice

00:05:58.104 --> 00:06:02.358
while deeply rooting our thoughts,
our words, our actions

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.944
in compassion and love?

00:06:08.114 --> 00:06:14.078
Táhirih's life teaches us that we must act
with urgency and conviction,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.041
while recognizing that we may not live
to see the fruits of our efforts.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:25.214
We cannot expect immediate gratification
in our pursuit of justice,

00:06:25.256 --> 00:06:30.303
because the transformation of society
is a multigenerational endeavor.

00:06:31.679 --> 00:06:33.056
So what can we do?

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.355
We can take to heart
Táhirih's trailblazing example,

00:06:40.396 --> 00:06:41.648
and in every moment,

00:06:41.689 --> 00:06:47.904
we too can bravely choose
to do the next right thing,

00:06:47.946 --> 00:06:53.201
knowing that we are planting seeds
that will grow into trees

00:06:53.242 --> 00:06:58.831
under whose branches future generations
will find justice and peace.

00:07:01.709 --> 00:07:04.045
I want to end with a poem of Táhirih's,

00:07:04.087 --> 00:07:06.881
translated from
its original language of Farsi,

00:07:07.966 --> 00:07:11.344
not only because it feels like
it could have been written today,

00:07:11.386 --> 00:07:14.514
in light of all the conflicts
we are witnessing with broken hearts

00:07:14.555 --> 00:07:16.307
all around the world,

00:07:16.349 --> 00:07:22.313
but also because she offers us
a clear and poignant call to action

00:07:22.355 --> 00:07:24.857
at a time where
we might be asking ourselves

00:07:24.899 --> 00:07:27.026
what we want our legacy to be.

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:36.369
"Let warring ways
be banished from the world.

00:07:37.787 --> 00:07:41.249
Let justice everywhere its carpet throw.

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:46.879
May friendship reconcile ancient hatreds.

00:07:46.921 --> 00:07:51.134
May love grow from the seed
of love we sow."

00:07:52.093 --> 00:07:53.386
Thank you.

00:07:53.428 --> 00:07:58.016
(Applause)

